    {
      "id": "fcmzmp8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "THE PREAMBLE OF THE INDIAN CONSTITUTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Preamble is based on the 'Objectives Resolution' drafted and moved by Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru on December 13, 1946.",
        "It was amended only once by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976, adding three new words: Socialist, Secular, and Integrity.",
        "The Preamble declares India to be a Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, and Republic state.",
        "It sets forth four key principles/objectives: Justice (Social, Economic, Political), Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity.",
        "The Preamble is non-justiciable, meaning its provisions are not enforceable in courts of law."
      ],
      "id": "eylqpth"
    },
